  <content>
  <section><title>The Effect of Gravity on Motion</title>
<para>A firework is sent up in the air, at an initial velocity of 130 feet per second, from a height of 6 feet. Write an equation that represents the height, \(h(t)\), of the firework for any second, \(t\). Remember to consider the effect of gravity.</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Identify the initial velocity, \(v_0\).</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Identify the starting height, \(h_0\).</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Write an equation of this form: \(h(t)=h_0+v_0t-16t^2\).</para>
<title>Try It: The Effect of Gravity on Motion</title>
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>Write an equation if a firework is shot from 2 feet off the ground at an initial velocity of 110 feet per second. Write an equation that represents the height, \(h(t)\), of the firework for any second, \(t\). Remember to consider the effect of gravity.</para>
  <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work. </para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Here is how to write the equation for the height of the firework:</para>
  <para><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Identify the initial velocity, \(v_0\).</para>
  <para><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Identify the starting height, \(h_0\).</para>
  <para><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Write an equation of this form: \(h(t)=h_0+v_0t-16t^2\).</para>
</solution></exercise>
</example></section>
